Source: pamsignal
Section: admin
Priority: optional
Maintainer: Tuan Nguyen <anhtuank7c@hotmail.com>
# Use the legacy `debhelper (>= 12~)` + `debian/compat` (file containing "13")
# form rather than `debhelper-compat (= 13)`. The latter is a standalone
# virtual package only published on Ubuntu 22.04+ (debhelper 13.x);
# Ubuntu 20.04 ships debhelper 12.10 which supports compat 13 via the
# legacy file but not the virtual build-dep. The two forms are
# interchangeable on Jammy/Noble/Resolute, so this single declaration covers
# Focal + everything newer.
Build-Depends: debhelper (>= 12~),
               meson,
               ninja-build,
               pkg-config,
               libsystemd-dev,
               libcmocka-dev
Standards-Version: 4.7.0
Homepage: https://github.com/anhtuank7c/pamsignal
Vcs-Git: https://github.com/anhtuank7c/pamsignal.git
Vcs-Browser: https://github.com/anhtuank7c/pamsignal
Rules-Requires-Root: no

Package: pamsignal
Architecture: any
Depends: ${shlibs:Depends},
         ${misc:Depends},
         curl,
         systemd
Pre-Depends: adduser
Description: Real-time PAM login monitor with multi-channel alerts
 PAMSignal monitors PAM authentication events via the systemd journal.
 It detects login attempts, logouts, and brute-force patterns from sshd,
 sudo, su, and login services, sending alerts to Telegram, Slack, Microsoft
 Teams, WhatsApp, Discord, or custom webhooks.
 .
 The daemon runs as an unprivileged system user with read-only access to
 the journal, isolates the alert dispatch path via fork+exec of curl, and
 ships with systemd hardening directives (NoNewPrivileges, ProtectSystem,
 SystemCallFilter, MemoryDenyWriteExecute, etc.).
